  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need final only. Need be thorough, complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls.\n\nNeed structure. Could use headings. Need ensure complete sentences. Avoid repeating. Let's plan.\n\nTitle: How a relational database executes a SQL query.\n\nSections:\n1. High-level pipeline: client sends SQL, server parses, validates, optimizes, executes, returns.\n2. Parsing and validation: lexical analysis, syntax tree, semantic analysis, name resolution, type checking, privilege checks, prepared statements. Example SELECT with join. Tradeoffs: prepared statement caching vs memory.\n3. Query rewriting and normalization: view expansion, common table expressions, subquery unnesting, predicate pushdown, constant folding. Example WHERE EXISTS vs IN. Pitfalls: subqueries that force materialization, volatile functions.\n4. Optimization/planning: logical plan, physical plan, cost model, statistics, cardinality estimates, access paths. Explain plan. Example indexes. Tradeoffs: cost model approximations, stale stats, optimizer hints.\n5. Indexes: B-tree, hash, GIN/GiST, covering, composite, partial, expression. How used: seek, range, index-only scan, bitmap heap scan. Example orders table. Tradeoffs: write amplification, maintenance, selectivity, index bloat, false positives? Pitfalls: leading wildcard, functions on indexed column, implicit casts, low selectivity, over-indexing, index-only scan visibility map.\n6. Joins: nested loop, hash join, merge join, index nested loop. Example orders/customers. Tradeoffs: memory, sorting, cardinality, skew. Pitfalls: cross joins, fanout, non-equi joins, join order, materialized subqueries.\n7. Aggregation/grouping/sorting: hash aggregate, sort, window functions. Tradeoffs memory/disk. Pitfalls: distinct, order by, temp files.\n8. Transactions and concurrency: MVCC, snapshots, locks, isolation levels, WAL, commit. How query sees data. Example read committed vs repeatable read.",
